//! The merge command contract (temper/docs/design/protocol/documentation.md, 8).
use std::fmt::Write;

/// The merge command's contract (temper/docs/design/protocol/tooling.md, 4.1).
pub(super) fn command(out: &mut String) {
    out.push_str("\n## The merge command\n\n`merge_job` names this command, a path in the repository being merged. It settles the conflicts its project's rules know, with no model, before an agent is asked. temper takes it from the merge in progress: a command file left in conflict, or absent from the merge, is its failure.\n\n- **What it is given:** its arguments, `merge` and the path of `request.json`, which holds `{\"version\":1,\"head\":\"HEAD\",\"base\":\"BASE\"}`: the head merged into, and the commit merged in. Its environment is `PATH` (`");
    out.push_str(std::str::from_utf8(temper_service_core::limits::TOOLING_PATH).expect("tooling PATH is UTF-8"));
    out.push_str("`), `LANG` (`C.UTF-8`), and `HOME` and `TEMPER_JOB`, both the job's directory, which is its working directory too. No credential reaches it. It has ");
    write!(out, "{}", temper_service_core::limits::MERGE_DEADLINE.as_nanos() / 1_000_000_000).expect("string");
    out.push_str(" seconds.\n- **Where it works:** the repository is the directory of its own name under `TEMPER_JOB`, at the head with the base merged in and not committed; the conflicting files are marked and unmerged in the index (`git diff --name-only --diff-filter=U`).\n- **What it must do:** edit the conflicting files it knows and `git add` each it settles, without committing, aborting the merge or writing another branch; then write `result.json` in `TEMPER_JOB` as compact JSON, at most ");
    write!(out, "{}", temper_service_core::limits::MERGE_RESULT_BYTES).expect("string");
    out.push_str(" bytes including its newline. Fit `left`'s records first, in index order, then `settled`'s paths in that order: each whole or omitted, with every one after it in its list omitted too. Write members `settled`, `left`, then `more`, omitting empty `left` and, unless anything was omitted, `more`. Where anything was omitted, end with `\"more\":{\"left\":N,\"settled\":M,\"read\":\"git status --short; git log --name-status --diff-filter=DR HEAD...MERGE_HEAD\"}`, N and M the counts omitted. The same head and base must give the same tree and result.\n- **Exit 0:** `{\"settled\":[PATH]}`, no file left unmerged: temper commits the tree as the merge, with both parents, and pushes it. A file left unmerged or still marked fails the job.\n- **Exit 3:** `{\"settled\":[PATH],\"left\":[LEFT]}`, each LEFT `{\"path\":PATH,\"kind\":KIND}`, KIND `modified`, `added`, `deleted`, `renamed` or `unknown`, read from the index stages and each side's history, not a missing stage alone. A deletion or one-sided rename adds `by` (`head` or `base`), `from` or `to` for a rename, and `commit`, the first twelve hexadecimal digits of the commit in that side's history that deleted or renamed it. A rename on both sides has `by` (`both`) alone. The head is the branch merged into; the base is the commit merged in. It settles no file one side deleted or renamed: the agent judges it. Nothing is committed or pushed; an agent resolves in a merge made afresh, temper carrying this result into its words as it is, never parsing it.\n- **Any other exit,** or a result missing or past its bound, is the command's failure. A job that fails for good holds the change, as a resolution that fails does.\n");
}
